<article class="env-proposition" id="068L">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/068L">Proposition <span data-tag="068L">9.2.7.12</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a small $\infty $-category. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is finitely dominated if and only if it is compact when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is finitely dominated, then it is a retract (in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$) of an essentially finite $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. Since $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is a compact object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ (Proposition <a data-tag="068G" href="/tag/068G">9.2.7.9</a>), it follows that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is also a compact object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ (Remark <a data-tag="064Z" href="/tag/064Z">9.2.5.18</a>). 
    <p>
      For the converse, we proceed as in the proof of Proposition <a data-tag="067X" href="/tag/067X">9.2.6.7</a>. Using Proposition <a data-tag="01BP" href="/tag/01BP">4.1.3.2</a>, we can choose a functor $Q: \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}\rightarrow \operatorname{QCat}$ which preserves small filtered colimits, which carries each simplicial set $X$ to an $\infty $-category $Q(X)$ equipped with an inner anodyne map $u_ X: X \hookrightarrow Q(X)$. Using Corollary <a data-tag="03DG" href="/tag/03DG">9.1.6.3</a>, we see that the functor 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}) \xrightarrow {Q} \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{QCat}) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{QCat}) = \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}} \]
</div>
<p>
       also preserves small filtered colimits. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a compact object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$, it follows that the functor 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}\rightarrow \operatorname{Set}\quad \quad X \mapsto \pi _0(\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, Q(X) ) )= \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, Q(X) ) \]
</div>
<p>
       preserves small filtered colimits. In particular, the equivalence $u_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow Q( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ factors up to homotopy through $Q( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 )$ for some finite simplicial subset $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. It follows that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a retract of the essentially finite $\infty $-category $Q( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 )$, and is therefore finitely dominated. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
